# Shell scripts must keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites
# apps/webuiapps/script/build.sh to CRLF, and the Docker build fails
# inside the node:20-alpine builder with:
#
#   ./script/build.sh: line 2: : not found
#   ./script/build.sh: set: line 3: illegal option -
#
# The `\r` becomes part of each token, so `set -e` parses as `set -e\r`.
# Linux and macOS checkouts never see this, which is why CI stays green
# while `make -C pmoves up-openroom` fails on a Windows fleet node.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else executed by a shell inside a container.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# REFRESHING AN EXISTING CHECKOUT.
#
# These attributes fix every FUTURE checkout and do nothing to a working tree
# that already exists: git will not rewrite a file it considers unchanged. A
# clone made before this commit keeps whatever bytes it has -- and `docker
# build` reads the WORKING TREE as its context, not the stored blob, so a stale
# CRLF copy still breaks the builder image.
#
# `git add --renormalize .` -- the sequence in the gitattributes docs -- does
# not help here. Measured on this branch it changes 0 files, because the blobs
# are already LF. The index was never the problem.
#
# On a node that already has this repo checked out, drop the affected file from
# the index and take it again, which is scoped and leaves other local work
# alone:
#
#     git rm --cached apps/webuiapps/script/build.sh
#     git checkout    apps/webuiapps/script/build.sh
#
# Verified on a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true: with the attributes
# present, build.sh checks out as 16 LF / 0 CRLF, because attributes override
# core.autocrlf.
#
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
